**ARTICLE 6: MONITORING, EVALUATION, AND LEARNING**

6.1 The Grantee shall establish and maintain a Project Monitoring, Evaluation, and Learning (MEL) system that is consistent with the Fund’s MEL Framework, a copy of which has been provided to the Grantee. The MEL system shall include, at minimum:

(a) A results framework with indicators, baselines, and targets for each output and outcome stated in the approved Project Proposal;

(b) A data collection plan specifying the methods, frequency, and responsibility for collecting data on each indicator;

(c) A data quality assurance procedure to ensure that all reported data are accurate, complete, consistent, and timely;

(d) A learning agenda that documents lessons learned and adaptive management decisions, to be reviewed quarterly.

6.2 The Grantee shall submit to the Fund a **Quarterly Progress Report** within fifteen (15) working days after the end of each calendar quarter. Each report shall include:

(a) A narrative summary of activities implemented, achievements against targets, and challenges encountered;

(b) A data table reporting actual values against the approved indicators for the reporting period and cumulatively;

(c) A financial summary comparing actual expenditures against the approved Budget, including any variances and explanations;

(d) A summary of community feedback received and actions taken in response;

(e) An updated risk register and mitigation measures.

6.3 The Grantee shall submit a **Final Report** no later than sixty (60) calendar days after the end of the Project period. The Final Report shall cover the entire Project period and shall include a final statement of expenditures, a final data table for all indicators, a sustainability plan, and an inventory of all assets procured with Grant funds.

6.4 The Fund, or its authorised representatives, shall have the right to conduct site visits and periodic audits of the Project. The Grantee shall facilitate such visits by providing reasonable access to Project sites, personnel, and records, and by making available appropriate staff for interviews. The Fund shall provide the Grantee with at least five (5) working days’ written notice of any site visit, except in cases of suspected fraud or misuse of funds, where notice may be shorter.

6.5 The Grantee shall maintain complete and accurate records relating to the Project, including financial records, attendance registers, training materials, procurement documents, and correspondence, for a period of at least five (5) years after the end of the Project period or until the conclusion of any audit, whichever is later. Such records shall be made available to the Fund, its auditors, or any authorised government oversight body upon request.

6.6 The Grantee shall ensure that all data collected under the Project are disaggregated by sex, age, and disability status, where feasible, to support the Fund’s commitment to inclusive development.

**ARTICLE 7: REPORTING AND COMMUNICATION**

7.1 All reports and communications required under this Agreement shall be submitted in English, in electronic format (PDF and editable spreadsheet for data tables), to the Fund’s designated Programme Officer at the address set out in Clause 1.2, or to such other address as the Fund may notify in writing.

7.2 The Grantee shall designate a single point of contact for all reporting and communication matters and shall notify the Fund of any change to that designation within ten (10) working days.
